<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anti-Revolutionary Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as ARP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anti-Revolutionary Party was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Protestant political party founded in 1879 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-kuyper">Abraham Kuyper</a>, the first national mass party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Rejecting the principles of the French Revolution, it fought for state funding of religious schools, won in 1917, and took part in many coalition governments. In 1980 it merged with Catholic and other Protestant parties to form the Christian Democratic Appeal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anti-revolutionary-party.jpg" alt="Anti-Revolutionary Party" width="320" height="124" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: ARP Vector: Strepulah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anti-Revolutionaire_Partij_logo_(1968-1980).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the mid-19th century the historian Guillaume Groen van Prinsterer laid the foundations of an Orthodox Reformed body of political thought that opposed popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and rationalism, and his followers took the label antirevolutionary. In parliament they were a small group, often three or four members. After Groen withdrew, the theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-kuyper">Abraham Kuyper</a> took up the leadership, founding the newspaper De Standaard in 1872. An 1878 petition for equal payment for religious schools helped bring about the party's formal founding on 3 April 1879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>To win funding for religious schools Kuyper allied with the Catholics, a strategy justified by the idea of the Antithesis, which held that the divide between secular and confessional parties outweighed the rivalry between Protestants and Catholics. In 1888 the party won 27 seats and Aeneas Mackay Jr. led a confessional cabinet. A dispute over the extension of suffrage split the party: Alexander de Savornin Lohman and many aristocratic members left, and their group became the Christian Historical Union in 1904. Kuyper himself led the cabinet from 1901 to 1905, when he pushed harsh anti-strike laws through parliament after the railway strike of 1903.</p><p>Hendrikus Colijn, leader from 1922, stressed defence and fiscal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> and led several cabinets between 1933 and 1939. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> members of the party served in the government-in-exile under Pieter Sjoerds Gerbrandy and in the resistance, where they founded the paper Trouw. Its opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> kept it out of government from 1946 until 1952. Under Willem Aantjes in the 1970s it took on a left-leaning radical evangelical image, and in 1974 it formed a federation with the KVP and CHU that campaigned as the Christian Democratic Appeal in 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party's thought rested on sphere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>: Protestants were to build a society of their own within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, with their own schools, papers and hospitals, funded equally by the state. Its motto was God, the Netherlands and the House of Orange. It drew first on the kleine luyden, the small folk of the middle class, farmers and workers, and later chiefly on members of the Reformed Churches in the Netherlands. It was strongest in Friesland, Overijssel, Zeeland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluwe">Veluwe</a>, and stood within a Protestant pillar that included the broadcaster NCRV and the Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam, founded in 1880.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anti-Revolutionary Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politics was dominated by confessional parties, including Abraham Kuyper’s <b>Anti-Revolutionary Party</b>, and since the Second World War by coalition governments of Christian democrats, social democrats and liberals, such as those of Willem Drees and, later, Ruud Lubbers, Wim Kok and Mark Rutte.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Netherlands, Abraham Kuyper founded the neo-Calvinist <b>Anti-Revolutionary Party</b>; in Germany the Lutheran Adolf Stoecker established the Christian Social Party.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/christian-democracy">Christian democracy · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
